#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "972bb75e-a787-58f1-83c0-1ae32aaf925d" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Points in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "c2883f5a-2126-51d9-b7bd-04bfd4418a3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7841" :question/problem "Find the image of the point $(-5,2)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $-3$?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-3x,-3y)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(-5,2)$, we have\n\n$$\n(-5,2) \\mapsto \\big((-3) \\cdot (-5), \\: (-3) \\cdot 2 \\big) = (15,-6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(15,-6)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261176ed-19bf-5d71-86b7-ba2e0ba31dc1" :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Figures in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "67b906da-2da2-570c-8a11-de5839c7686a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7842" :question/problem "![](images/83/83e7c4533710e42e16cbe883188f619321914815d905344547bd6c81d253a3ac.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-2$?" :question/worked-solution "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-2$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,-2y\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(2,1)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,3)$, $(1,3)$, $(1,2)$, and $(2,2)$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2, 1) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 2,-2 \\cdot 1) = (-4,-2) \\\\ (3, 1) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 3,-2 \\cdot 1) = (-6,-2) \\\\ (3, 3) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 3,-2 \\cdot 3) = (-6,-6) \\\\ (1, 3) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 1,-2 \\cdot 3) = (-2,-6) \\\\ (1, 2) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 1,-2 \\cdot 2) = (-2,-4) \\\\ (2, 2) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 2,-2 \\cdot 2) = (-4,-4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/a3/a3ce2ccd16db3e9013947b475bbca5fec1d34ff5e10414eff426e8389271c219.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078369 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f22cdd47-6fd4-5f15-b5a9-ce290e204a57" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factor of a Dilation in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "7c567754-a661-5235-bde3-ae6cbff72ed5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7843" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd07f68993f7e151e2b24fe9305b726ce40b9bc5f56399d6616e46c83f90cc87.png)\n\nThe point $P'(-2,-4)$ is the image of the point $P(1,2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,$.\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P'$, we get\n\n$$\n(k \\cdot 1, k \\cdot 2) = (-2,-4)⟹{k =-2; 2k =-4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078373 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2ce690-f8a9-5cc2-9baf-2d7163602589" :knowledge-point/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"}] :question/id #uuid "b0ca5c59-6d06-5b5a-8b25-bd364fcb4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8547" :question/problem "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right)$. What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation maps a line $l$ not passing through the center of the dilation onto a parallel line $l'$.\n\nTo find the image of the line, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Take a point $B \\in l$.\n- Find the image $B'$ of $B$ under the dilation.\n- Find $l'$ as the line that passes through $B'$ and is parallel to $l$.\n\nNotice that $B(-3,0)$ lies on $l$, as shown below. The image of this point under the dilation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot (-3), \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot 0) &= (-5, 0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following image of $l$:\n\n![](images/7e/7eca5ac26e32d153f69f67076100c7c1ebc26efe26d5b1603ed459a9101f93db.png)"}]}
